Based on the search results, here's what I found about SD_MMC one-bit mode configuration for ESP32-S3:

## SD_MMC One-Bit Mode Implementation

You can use 1-bit line mode (CLK, CMD, D0, 3.3V, GND) by passing the second argument mode1bit==true.

## Pin Configuration Method

The SD_MMC library provides the `setPins(int clk, int cmd, int d0)` function for one-bit mode configuration.

## Arduino Sketch Example Structure

Here's how to implement this in an Arduino sketch based on the examples found:

```cpp
#include "FS.h"
#include "SD_MMC.h"

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Define your pins for XIAO ESP32-S3 Sense
  int clk = 36;   // Clock pin
  int cmd = 35;   // Command pin
  int d0 = 37;    // Data 0 pin

  // Set pins for 1-bit mode
  if (!SD_MMC.setPins(clk, cmd, d0)) {
    Serial.println("Pin change failed!");
    return;
  }

  // Initialize SD_MMC
  if (!SD_MMC.begin()) {
    Serial.println("Card Mount Failed");
    return;
  }

  Serial.println("SD Card initialized successfully!");
}

void loop() {
  // Your code here
}
```

**Note:** ESP32-S3-WROOM-2 uses most default GPIOs (33-37) to interface with on-board OPI flash, and if SD_MMC is initialized with default pins it will result in a rebooting loop - please reassign the pins elsewhere using the setPins command. Make sure to verify the correct pin assignments for your specific XIAO ESP32-S3 Sense board.


